WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.167 --> 00:00:05.085
(television static)
(Netflix logo sound effect)

00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:07.752
(ambient music)

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:10.303
- [Vincent] My friends,

00:00:10.303 --> 00:00:11.580
when I told them about the records I had,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.407
they were saying, "So you had Bananarama

00:00:13.407 --> 00:00:16.440
"and you had Erasure and
you had George Michael."

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.840
They said to me, "Did
nobody know you were gay?"

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.727
My name's Vincent Creelan,
I live in Newtownards,

00:00:25.727 --> 00:00:27.160
which is a small town in Northern Ireland.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.070
I got married when I was in
university, when I was 19.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.230
I thought getting married
would maybe straighten me out.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.470
I knew, six months in, it wasn't.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.440
I was still having a wank,

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:37.650
thinking about men, to be honest with you,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.643
so I knew that strategy had failed.

00:00:40.538 --> 00:00:41.770
In Northern Ireland, being gay,

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.030
there were just no positive messages.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.730
I suppose the big realization was,

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.990
I was about 15, I think, in my bedroom.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:49.700
I can't really remember the radio station,

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.740
but this guy was being interviewed

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.897
talking about being in a
relationship with another guy.

00:00:52.897 --> 00:00:54.427
"I wanna tell you this," he says,

00:00:54.427 --> 00:00:57.167
"All across UK, there are
young guys listening to this.

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:59.617
"They're hearing something
that means something to them,

00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:02.040
"and they're really worried
that father or somebody

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.711
will walk into the bedroom and hear them."

00:01:03.711 --> 00:01:04.740
That was me.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.473
And that's when I realized that
this was kind of telling me

00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:08.730
that I was gay and that the fears I had

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.830
and the concerns I had
and the isolation I had

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.893
was very real and I wasn't alone.

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:17.180
The first book I bought,
which would've been,

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.190
I suppose gay literature,

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.350
would've been A Boy's Own
Story by Edmund White.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:21.450
That was the first time I read something

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.000
that I could put myself in that position,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.920
have those same feelings
and the same crushes

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.490
he talked about on boys.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.790
I remember reading it,
not reading it in secret

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.390
but not really saying
what the book was about.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.040
There wasn't really much
for me to connect with

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.440
for a long, long time.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.973
I just had to pick out
characters and people

00:01:37.973 --> 00:01:41.233
that I either thought might
be gay or that I fancied.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.370
I came off night shift,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.200
a film called My Beautiful
Laundrette was on.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.630
I thought, "Well, my wife's at work."

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.950
I realized this was a gay film
and I just had to watch it.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.830
It felt very real for the way things were

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.810
in the world at that stage
and it was passionate

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.260
and there was kissing
and there was moments

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.300
where they just lost control
almost and took risks.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.810
I was doing none of that, obviously,

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.660
but I looked at it and thought
that's what it could be,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.050
and that's what I wanted.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.480
I remember there was a
character in Eastenders.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.304
I'm just thinking about
the kiss and it was huge.

00:02:09.304 --> 00:02:10.137
I think they locked lips just about,

00:02:10.137 --> 00:02:11.310
and then there's a whole fury

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:13.498
they report in the news, oh,
the BBC got so many complaints

00:02:13.498 --> 00:02:14.810
and it was nothing. And yet,

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.710
you see some work where
heterosexual love and intimacy

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:22.030
The year I came out, it was 1997, 1998.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.267
There still wasn't a lot to
access, to be honest with you,

00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:26.930
and that was before Queer As Folk.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.400
You didn't really have the internet.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.540
Most of the stuff that was on television

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.940
was still those camp comedic
characters or performers.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.200
But that changed, I
guess, within five years.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.820
Queer As Folk seemed to open a door

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.610
and I think for everybody
the first episode,

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.401
it kind of set the tone for it.

00:02:42.401 --> 00:02:43.790
The young guy goes out on the scene,

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.060
everyone was gay. Gay friends and

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.740
they were just out for a night together

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.840
and this young guy was looking for sex.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.740
Next thing you know, he's copping off

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.130
and then he's getting naked
and then he's getting rimmed.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.533
It was exciting, it was like
this is where it could be.

00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:01.533
The very first time seeing as
close as you can to sex on TV,

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:02.870
and it was this big show and

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.090
everybody was kind of talking about it.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:06.600
I tend to watch quite a bit of Netflix,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.830
because that's where I go
for my series like Star Trek

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.230
or What If or some of the other
series that we've watched.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.960
And you find quite often by accident

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.000
that there is a gay character
or there's a gay couple

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.693
or there's a gay theme in it.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:20.980
One of the programs I recently watched

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.850
was the Tales of the City.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.950
You always wonder when
something's re-filmed or remade,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.760
is it really gonna stand the test?

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.400
Seeing how gay life progressed,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.660
and then seeing some of the
characters being matured

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:33.873
and some of them are in relationships.

00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:36.053
That was really good, I really enjoyed it.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.510
I loved to watch Grace and Frankie.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.420
Some of the truths with
their conversations,

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.140
and some of the banter,
everyone was kindof

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.670
settling into the friendships
and into the relationships.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.200
It was lovely seeing
an older man coming out

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.033
within a very short time he found

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:49.866
all these gay people, he found

00:03:49.866 --> 00:03:50.900
these gay groups to be involved in,

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.740
and he had this kindof gay life.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.430
It's good sometimes having a comedy

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.530
in which the characters
are just being themselves

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.330
rather than where the gay character

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:00.890
has to be something bizarre and ridiculous

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.550
and shocking all the time.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.250
It's good to see you reflected in a drama,

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.710
whether its a policing
drama, a science fiction one

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:10.470
even documentaries.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.790
I liked seeing the life
that I perceive to be mine

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.120
which I still think
being gay or being queer

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:17.370
is a very different life.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.340
I think you just get used to it and forget

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.890
how quickly it has changed
in maybe five years.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.970
Because certainly when I
came out ten years ago,

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.360
it just, you were seeing caricatures,

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.960
comedian representations of
what straight people thought

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.180
you should see what was acceptable.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.480
And now it seems that
there is an acceptance,

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.962
not a toleration, there is an acceptance.

00:04:37.962 --> 00:04:41.300
A kind of celebration of what my life

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.380
and loads of other peoples lives

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.270
in what is probably one
of the largest minorities

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.690
in our societies and I have been seeing it

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.843
in social media and TV and films.

